---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Dev / Scan Process"
  description="How Tyler Walker develops, camera-scans, converts, and prepares film photographs for the web."
>
  <header class="page-head process-head">
    <h1>Dev / Scan Process</h1>
    <p>
      This is my typical workflow. Individual rolls may use a different lab,
      chemistry, or scanning approach; when that context matters, I'll note it
      alongside the roll.
      </p>
      <p>
      I've started developing myself but I've also used:
      <ul>
        <li>Moody's Film Lab in Burien, WA</li>
        <li>We Develop Film in Edmonds, WA</li>
        <li>Panda Lab in Seattle, WA</li> 
        <li>The Darkroom</li>
        <li>Color House in New York, NY</li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </header>

  <ol class="process-steps">
    <li class="process-step">
      <span class="process-step__number" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Development</h2>
        <p>
          Development is part of the image-making process, not an invisible
          mechanical step. Film stock, chemistry, time, temperature, and the
          lab's handling all affect contrast, grain, color, and density.
        </p>
        <p>
          I keep the negatives as the source of record and document the lab or
          development process on the roll whenever that information is useful.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li class="process-step">
      <span class="process-step__number" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Negative Preparation</h2>
        <p>
          Before scanning, I inspect the film and remove loose dust with clean,
          non-contact tools. The negative is loaded into a VALOI easy120 carrier,
          which keeps both 35mm and medium-format film flat and consistently
          positioned above the light source.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li class="process-step">
      <span class="process-step__number" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Camera Scanning</h2>
        <p>
          I digitize each frame with a Nikon Zf. The camera is aligned square to
          the negative, focus and exposure are set manually, and the files are
          captured in RAW so the negative's available color and tonal information
          remains intact.
        </p>
        <p>
          The goal at this stage is a sharp, evenly illuminated reproduction—not
          a finished positive.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li class="process-step">
      <span class="process-step__number" aria-hidden="true">04</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Conversion</h2>
        <p>
          RAW captures are brought into Adobe Lightroom Classic and converted
          with Negative Lab Pro. I establish a neutral starting point from the
          film border, crop the frame, and let the conversion preserve as much
          useful highlight and shadow information as possible.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li class="process-step">
      <span class="process-step__number" aria-hidden="true">05</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Edit &amp; Cleanup</h2>
        <p>
          Every scan or darkroom print is an interpretation. I adjust color,
          density, contrast, and framing to reach the photograph I intended—not
          to pretend that a scan arrived without choices.
        </p>
        <p>
          Cleanup is generally limited to dust and physical defects from the
          scanning process. I want the photograph to remain a photograph.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li class="process-step">
      <span class="process-step__number" aria-hidden="true">06</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Web Output</h2>
        <p>
          Finished masters are exported as high-quality JPEGs. The site's ingest
          tool stores a web master no larger than 2600 pixels on the long edge at
          JPEG quality 86. During the site build, Astro creates responsive WebP
          versions so visitors download only the size their screen needs.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <aside class="process-note">
    <h2>Why describe the process?</h2>
    <p>
      Film does not remove editing; it moves those decisions into development,
      printing, scanning, and conversion. Sharing the process makes those choices
      visible and gives each roll a little more context.
    </p>
  </aside>
</Base>
